, 002, 006, 009, 012) already have results in the registry, while the truly pending work is the Plan-016 cross-family validation experiments that are currently running. Since the user asked specifically for data on each planned experiment, I should pull the actual results from those five completed experiments rather than making predictions about the Plan-016 ones. I need to generate predicted results for all the planned and running experiments under the best-case scenario defined by the best tier. I'll create detailed YAML output covering the 5 planned experiments from experiment_plan plus the 5 plan-016 experiments from the registry, with predictions that are internally consistent with the best tier definition and include rationales for each.\n\nNow I'm laying out the specific metric ranges for the best tier — binary LOGO F1 should hit between 0.71 and 0.74 across 9 generators and 6 families, with the 7-feature advantage showing 2.5 to 5 percentage points over the best single detector, and new family held-out F1 ranging from 0.68 to 0.78. I'm also tracking the per-generator F1 standard deviation, worst single generator performance, and ensuring the differential response pattern between drift and stationary conditions replicates consistently across new families.\n\nFor the feature ablation experiment, I'm predicting that the full model will reach around 0.82 macro F1, with reference-only features at 0.73 and surface-only at 0.77, creating roughly a 5 percentage point gap that demonstrates reference features add meaningful signal. The ablation should clearly isolate entropy_mean, TTR, and hapax_ratio as the strongest H2 features while confirming tail_decay and entropy as key H1 contributors.\n\nMoving to cross-generator evaluation with 9 generators, I expect the leave-one-out average to land around 0.72 for binary classification and 0.79 for three-class, with per-generator variation around 0.05. The worst performer likely drops to 0.67 while the best reaches 0.85. Perplexity shows a consistent monotonic decline across depth levels, and the paraphrastic comparison should demonstrate clear advantages for continuation chains over paraphrastic alternatives. For the finer-grained 5-class ordinal task, performance remains solid under the best tier conditions.\n\nNow I'm focusing on the plan-016 experiments with Llama, Gemma, and Mistral—I need to predict generation completion metrics, feature extraction correlations, held-out LOGO F1 scores, and whether the differential response pattern replicates. The Qwen8B cross-reference is essentially complete with metrics already in the registry, and the Llama 3.2 3B variant serves as a diagnostic backup. Let me structure the comprehensive YAML output with precise predictions for each experiment.\n\nI'm noticing that several experiments marked as \"planned\" in the experiment plan have actually been completed and logged in the registry—plan-001 through plan-012 all have corresponding entries. Looking at what's genuinely still running, I see plan-016-llama32-3b and plan-016-gemma2-2b are actively executing, with more queued behind them.\n\nThe user asked specifically for planned experiments from the YAML, but since plan-016 is heavily referenced in the tier definitions and has multiple active runs, I should include those alongside the originally planned ones. Even though some of the earlier plans show as \"planned\" but are already done, I'll still generate predictions for all of them since that's what was requested.\n\nNow I'm working through each experiment to construct the best-tier scenario results. Starting with plan_001 on feature ablation—in the best case, the full feature set shows clear advantages over individual subsets, with reference features providing genuine complementary signal rather than just filling vocabulary gaps. I'd expect the full model to reach around 0.82 macro F1, surface-only around 0.76, and reference-only around 0.72, with clean separation between the drifting and stationary hypotheses.\n\nFor the statistical breakdown, the drifting features show strong monotonic relationships with depth—entropy mean drops sharply (slope -0.28, R² 0.96), type-token ratio and hapax ratio both decline consistently—while stationary features remain largely invariant with minimal slopes and low R² values. Perplexity decreases monotonically across depths, confirming the location-shift model where mean NLL drops steadily, causing diversity metrics to collapse while shape features stay stable.\n\nNow I'm evaluating cross-generator performance with 9 generators across 6 families using leave-one-out validation. The 3-class F1 drops slightly to around 0.79 compared to the 6-generator baseline of 0.789, and binary classification F1 lands in the 0.71-0.74 range—I need to reconcile this with the earlier 6-generator binary F1 of 0.723 to see how much the additional generators degrade performance. Looking at the per-generator performance metrics, I'm checking whether the new model families (Llama, Gemma, Mistral) fall within the expected ranges. Their average held-out F1 of 0.717 fits nicely in the [0.68, 0.78] band, and I'm adjusting the worst performer down slightly to 0.685 to stay within the [0.65, 0.7] constraint.\n\nWith nine generators in the training mix instead of six, Qwen3-8B should see a modest boost from the increased diversity, pushing it up to around 0.695, while Gemma's domain shift from the relative position parameter keeps it as the weakest link at 0.68.\n\nNow I'm calculating the per-generator F1 statistics to verify everything stays within the target ranges—the new family average comes to 0.71, and the standard deviation across all nine generators should confirm the spread is reasonable.\n\nFor the binary LOGO F1 score, I'm aiming for 0.73, which gives a 3 percentage point advantage over the best single detector baseline in the 9-generator setup, fitting nicely within the expected range.\n\nNow looking at the paraphrastic comparison results: continuation chains show stronger depth signal than paraphrastic ones, with F1 scores of 0.79 versus 0.74 respectively—a 5 point gap that aligns with the theoretical prediction that non-paraphrastic chains preserve more of the depth information. The cross-transfer performance around 0.75 and consistent Spearman signs across both chain types validate this distinction.\n\nMoving to finer-grained classification, the 5-class setup reveals a hard ceiling—F1 drops to 0.51 compared to 0.79 for 3-class, confirming that gen-2 and gen-3 are fundamentally inseparable at this granularity. This supports the paper's core argument that while depth estimation is meaningful, it has natural practical limits.\n\nThe end-to-end QLoRA fine-tuned model performs substantially better, reaching 0.90 F1 on 3-class versus the 0.79 from XGBoost, though the 5-class performance still plateaus around 0.59. The learned representations capture more signal, but this doesn't shift the narrative—interpretable features remain the stronger contribution for the paper.\n\nNow testing with Llama 3.2 3B as a new model family to see how generation and feature extraction perform across this architecture.\n\nAfter filtering, I'm looking at roughly 4500 records with a degenerate rate under 3% and the expected slight attrition at deeper levels. The feature correlations show hapax_ratio, TTR, and entropy_mean all drifting with depth (ranging from -0.52 to -0.45), while entropy_std and tail_decay remain stationary. The held-out LOGO F1 comes in at 0.72.\n\nSwitching to Gemma 2 2B from Google, though the previous run at rp=1.0 hit a 10% degenerate rate, so I'll need to adjust.\n\nFor Mistral 7B, the picture is cleaner—about 4400 records with only 1.2% degenerates, strong depth signal across the board, and F1 of 0.73. With Qwen 8B as the reference model, the entropy drift pattern holds consistently across all references with minimal spread, and the Qwen-ref LOGO variant performs best.\n\nThe Llama 3.2 3B variant with rp=1.1 is mainly a diagnostic check to see how fewer degenerates affect the results, not a primary focus.\n\nNow I'm structuring the full YAML output with detailed rationales and including the analysis descriptions, tables, and figures for each experiment.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3726,"cache_read_input_tokens":42472,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3726},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"670f01e8-9a77-4683-a438-fb4d9dd8cb5c","uuid":"eff5bd3f-684b-4fb1-a673-d45e59fa2c91"}
