{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Q181j7S9RyL3t1WY9JVcQy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the project. Let me construct the comprehensive best-tier predictions for all planned experiments.\n\nLet me identify the full list:\n\nFrom experiment_plan (status: planned):\n1. plan_001 - feature_ablation_and_trend\n2. plan_002 - cross_generator_evaluation\n3. plan_006 - paraphrastic_comparison\n4. plan_009 - finer_grained_ordinal\n5. plan_012 - end_to_end_classifier\n\nFrom registry (RUNNING/QUEUED):\n6. plan-016-llama32-3b\n\n\n7. plan-016-gemma2-2b\n8. plan-016-mistral-7b\n9. plan-016-crossref-qwen8b\n10. plan-016-llama32-3b-rp11\n\nFor the best tier predictions, I need to maintain consistency across the key performance metrics: binary logo F1 scores around 0.71-0.74, per-generation F1 standard deviation between 0.04-0.07, worst single generation F1 in the 0.65-0.7 range, and seven-feature versus best baseline performance gain of 2.5-5.0 percentage points, while also accounting for the new family category.\n\nNow I'm looking at the held-out F1 performance across the range of 0.68-0.78, the drifting feature Spearman correlations spanning -0.7 to -0.35, and shape feature Spearman correlations clustering tightly between -0.12 and 0.12. The narrative confirms that Plan-016's new model families all demonstrate the depth signal consistently, cross-family generalization is fully established, and the paper's framing doesn't require adjustment.\n\nAs I work through the YAML structure, I'm focusing on crafting detailed result rationales for each experiment that explain the mechanistic reasons why best-tier performance would produce these particular outcomes. For plan-001's feature ablation and trend analysis, the baseline 5-generation MVP data shows the full 7-feature model achieving macro F1 around 0.82, while the surface-only variant using just TTR and hapax met